+## Review-8aee66b709b13528 — greedy nearest-checkpoint shortcut
+
+A reusable source-pinned protected-Racket probe was added at `workspace/probe_nearest_checkpoint_shortcut.py`. In the ordinary hard seed-3 four-demo window, greedily visiting the nearest remaining checkpoint (numeric tie break) exactly matches the canonical numeric checkpoint order in every demonstration. The existing `lesson_identifiable` checker reports no finding because this rival is absent from `LESSON_COMPETITORS`. On the next lawful query, canonical order is `[2,4,5,3]` while greedy order is `[2,5,4,3]`, changing ten protected-Racket output cells.
+
+Suggested repair: add the greedy-nearest executable competitor to `LESSON_COMPETITORS`, or require a visible demonstration where greedy and numeric order differ. Production remains unchanged; this is one ordinary source-bound lesson, not a universal schedule census. Evidence: `checks/df17e660cb8e/nearest-checkpoint-shortcut.json`.
